transcript.content:
For a population standard deviation of $\sigma = 12$ and sample size $n=100$:

$$
\text{SE}(\bar{x})=\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}
=\frac{12}{\sqrt{100}}
=\frac{12}{10}
=1.2\text{ units}
$$

So the standard error is **1.2 units**, not $0.6$.

Comparison:

- At $n=25$: $\text{SE} = 12/\sqrt{25} = 12/5 = 2.4$ units  
- At $n=100$: $\text{SE} = 12/\sqrt{100} = 12/10 = 1.2$ units  

The sample size became **four times larger** ($25 \to 100$), but $\sqrt{n}$ became only **twice as large** ($5 \to 10$). Therefore the standard error was cut **in half**: $2.4 \to 1.2$.

Exactly what got smaller: the **spread of the sampling distribution of sample means**—the histogram of repeated-sample averages. The fixed population’s standard deviation remains **12 units**.

