Since you've already mastered the difference between random samples and volunteers, let's put that intuition to work on a real-world constraint. You have a finite amount of time (6 hours) and a reliable sampling frame (the registered families list).

The challenge here is balancing the "purity" of the sample against the practical reality of getting a teenager to actually talk to you.

```openui lifecycle=ephemeral id=sampling-choice

root = LearningSurface([choice, justification], "Sampling Strategy: Community Centre", "Choose the most effective way to reach the teenagers.", "ephemeral")

choice = Question([
  { 
    question: "Which sampling plan will you implement?", 
    type: "choice", 
    choices: [
      "Plan A: Simple Random Sample. Randomly select 60 families from the list. Call each one and request to speak with the teenager. If they aren't home or refuse, move to the next name immediately.", 
      "Plan B: Stratified Sample. Divide the list into 'Attenders' and 'Non-Attenders'. Randomly select 30 from each group. Spend more time per call to encourage the non-attenders to participate, accepting fewer total completions."
    ], 
    hint: "Consider how the 'non-attender' group might behave differently when contacted." 
  }
], "ephemeral", "Sampling Design")

justification = Question([
  { 
    question: "Justify your choice: Why is this plan less biased or more feasible than the alternative? Also, name one specific downside of your chosen plan.", 
    type: "text", 
    hint: "Think about the 6-hour time limit and the likelihood of response from teenagers who already avoid the centre." 
  }
], "ephemeral", "Reasoning")
```

```openui lifecycle=resumable id=logic-foundations-deck

root = LearningSurface([deck], "Logic Foundations", "Quick retrieval practice for conditional reasoning.", "workspace")

deck = Flashcards(
  "logic-foundations-deck", 
  "Conditional Logic", 
  "Core concepts of logical implication and conditions", 
  [
    {
      id: "implication",
      front: "Implication (If P, then Q)",
      back: "Toy rule: 'If it is raining, the grass is wet.' If the first part (P) is true, the second (part Q) must also be true."
    },
    {
      id: "converse",
      front: "Converse",
      back: "Swapping the order: 'If the grass is wet, it is raining.' Note: The converse of a true implication is not necessarily true."
    },
    {
      id: "contrapositive",
      front: "Contrapositive",
      back: "Swapping and negating: 'If the grass is not wet, it is not raining.' This always has the same truth value as the original implication."
    },
    {
      id: "necessary",
      front: "Necessary Condition",
      back: "A requirement that must be met. Toy rule: 'Oxygen is necessary for fire.' If there is no oxygen, there can be no fire."
    },
    {
      id: "sufficient",
      front: "Sufficient Condition",
      back: "A condition that guarantees the outcome. Toy rule: 'Pouring a bucket of water on a fire is sufficient to put it out.'"
    },
    {
      id: "counterexample",
      front: "Counterexample to a Universal Claim",
      back: "A single case that proves a 'for all' statement false. Claim: 'All birds fly.' Counterexample: 'The penguin is a bird that does not fly.'"
    }
  ], 
  "workspace", 
  "Review the relationship between conditions and their logical transformations."
)
```

