{"topic": "Candle burning in a sealed container with mass change", "kind": "hyperframes", "summary": "Two competing models: wax becomes invisible gas vs. container gains mass from heat", "body": "<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com"></script>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/gsap@3.12.2/dist/gsap.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <div style="max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1rem; font-family: system-ui;">
    <h2 style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Two Explanations, One Container</h2>

    <div id="model-a" style="background: #fef3c7; border: 1px solid #f59e0b; padding: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;">
      <h4>Model A: Mass drops (wax → gas)</h4>
      <p>Combustion turns wax into hot gases (CO₂, H₂O vapor) that fill the container. The total mass inside stays the same as the wax consumed, but the wax "disappears" from view as it becomes transparent gas.</p>
      <p><strong>Key claim:</strong> The container feels lighter as wax is consumed.</p>
    </div>

    <div id="model-b" style="background: #dbeafe; border: 1px solid #3b82f6; padding: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;">
      <h4>Model B: Container gains mass from heat</h4>
      <p>Heat energy adds effective mass to the container walls via thermal excitation. The candle's light and heat transfer energy to the container, making it register as more mass on a scale.</p>
      <p><strong>Key claim:</strong> The container feels heavier due to accumulated heat.</p>
    </div>

    <div id="result" style="margin-top: 1rem; padding: 0.5rem; border-radius: 0.5rem; display: none; text-align: center;"></div>

    <script>
      // Simulate: both models can't be right simultaneously about mass change direction
      const totalMass = 100; // starting mass in grams
      const waxMass = 15; // wax that will burn

      // Model A: mass decreases as wax becomes gas (but gas is still in container)
      const modelAFinal = totalMass; // mass conserved, wax still in system

      // Model B: container gains mass from heat - this is physically wrong for closed system
      // but let's show the mechanism
      const heatGain = 0.5; // imagined thermal mass increase
      const modelBFinal = totalMass + heatGain;

      // The real answer: in a sealed container, mass is conserved
      // Wax → gases (CO2 + H2O) that stay inside = no net mass change

      document.getElementById('model-a').addEventListener('click', () => {
        showResult('Model A says: mass stays the same (gas still inside). Wax just becomes invisible.');
      });

      document.getElementModel-b').addEventListener('click', () => {
        showResult('Model B says: container gains mass from heat. This would violate conservation of mass.');
      });

      function showResult(msg) {
        document.getElementById('result').style.display = 'block';
        document.getElementById('result').innerHTML = '<p style="color: #dc2626;">' + msg + '</p>';
      }
    </script>
  </div>
</body>
</html>"}

