transcript.content:
Here's a single OpenUI study plan you can edit for learning to make an accessible recipe page. It's structured as four 25-minute sessions, one step per session. Everything stays local — no course or goal is saved unless you want it.

```openui lifecycle=workspace id=accessible-recipe-plan

root = LearningSurface([step1, step2, step3, step4], "Accessible Recipe Page — 4 Sessions", "Build an accessible recipe page in four 25-minute sessions. Each step tells you what to make and how to check it.", "workspace")

$session = 1

step1 = StudyPlan("session-1-headings", "Session 1 — Semantic headings", [
  { id: "make", title: "Add HTML5 document structure and recipe headings", detail: "Create a basic HTML5 page with <h1> for the recipe title, <h2> for sections (ingredients, instructions), and <h3> for ingredient subsections. Include a <nav> with skip links at the top.", estimatedMinutes: 25, outcomes: ["Page has a clear heading hierarchy starting with <h1>"]
  }, { id: "check", title: "Validate heading structure", detail: "Open the page in a browser and verify the heading order: <h1> → <h2> → <h3>. Use the browser's accessibility inspector (Chrome DevTools → Elements → Accessibility) to confirm the heading levels are announced correctly.", estimatedMinutes: 5, outcomes: ["Heading hierarchy is semantically correct"] }
], "workspace", "Step 1 of 4")

step2 = StudyPlan("session-2-ingredients", "Session 2 — Ingredient lists and keyboard navigation", [
  { id: "make", title: "Add an accessible ingredient list", detail: "Create an unordered list <ul> of ingredients. Each list item <li> should have the ingredient name followed by its amount. Wrap the entire list in a <section> with an <h2> label.", estimatedMinutes: 25, outcomes: ["Ingredient list is marked up as a semantic <ul> inside a <section>"]
  }, { id: "check", title: "Test keyboard navigation through the list", detail: "Tab through the ingredient items. Each focusable item should be reachable in order. Verify no keyboard traps exist. If you use labels or buttons inside list items, ensure they have clear focus styles (outline or background change).", estimatedMinutes: 5, outcomes: ["All ingredient items are reachable via Tab in a logical order"] }
], "workspace", "Step 2 of 4")

step3 = StudyPlan("session-3-instructions", "Session 3 — Accessible instructions and screen reader check", [
  { id: "make", title: "Add step-by-step instructions with ARIA landmarks", detail: "Create an ordered list <ol> of method steps. Each step <li> should describe one action. Add role=\"region\" with aria-label=\"Cooking instructions\" on the <ol>. Include a <footer> with a link back to the top of the page.", estimatedMinutes: 25, outcomes: ["Instructions are in an ordered list with ARIA region labeling"]
  }, { id: "check", title: "Run a screen reader check", detail: "Use your operating system's built-in screen reader (Windows: Narrator ⊞+Ctrl+S, macOS: VoiceOver ⌘+F5 or Trackpad Commander) to read the page. Listen that: the recipe title is announced, heading levels are correct, the ingredient list is announced as a list, and each instruction step is read in order. Note any missing announcements and adjust markup.", estimatedMinutes: 5, outcomes: ["Screen reader announces all structural elements and content in correct order"] }
], "workspace", "Step 3 of 4")

step4 = StudyPlan("session-4-final-check", "Session 4 — Final accessibility review and polish", [
  { id: "make", title: "Run a full accessibility review and fix remaining issues", detail: "Re-run the screen reader check from Session 3. Fix any missing alt text on images, ensure color contrast is adequate (use a contrast checker tool), and confirm all interactive elements have keyboard focus indicators. Add a simple <table> for nutrition facts if you have them, with <th> for headers.", estimatedMinutes: 25, outcomes: ["All major accessibility barriers are addressed; page passes basic a11y checks"]
  }, { id: "check", title: "Final verification and reflection", detail: "Summarize what you changed from the starting point. Write one sentence about what you learned about accessible markup. Keep the page as a reusable template for future recipes.", estimatedMinutes: 5, outcomes: ["You can explain one key principle of accessible web page structure"] }
], "workspace", "Step 4 of 4")

# Navigation between steps
prevBtn = Button("Previous step", Action([$set($session, @Max($session - 1, 1))]))
nextBtn = Button("Next step", Action([$set($session, @Min($session + 1, 4))]))

nav = Stack([prevBtn, nextBtn], "row", "l")

root = LearningSurface([step1, step2, step3, step4, nav], "Accessible Recipe Page — 4 Sessions", "Build an accessible recipe page in four 25-minute sessions. Each step tells you what to make and how to check it.", "workspace")
```

**How to use it:**
- Each `StudyPlan` has a `make` task (25 min) and a `check` task (≈5 min) for that session.
- The navigation buttons at the bottom let you move between steps.
- All content stays local in this chat — no course or goal is created unless you ask.

Let me know if you'd like to adjust any step, add more detail, or move on to practice activities like a quiz or flashcards for the concepts you've covered.

